\subsection{Function-Level Formulation Comparison}
\label{sec:equivalence}

Among the three formulations, \cls{} achieves the highest function-level accuracy: 88.4\% vs.\ \cwm{} 87.2\% (gap 1.24\pp{}, 5 seeds) and \traj{} 86.3\% (gap ${\sim}$2\pp{}, 3 paired seeds). The \cls{}--\cwm{} gap is within seed noise (0.02--1.24\pp{} across model families); the \cls{}--\traj{} gap is small but consistent across paired seeds. On DeepSeek-Coder, \cls{} likewise achieves the highest accuracy (86.9\% vs.\ \cwm{} 86.8\%, \traj{} 84.2\%). Within-problem predictions are correlated (ICC $= 0.191$, $p < 0.0001$, design effect (DEFF) $= 3.97$); all statistical tests use clustered bootstrap accordingly. Per-test classification provides diagnostic information (which specific tests fail) at the highest accuracy among formulations, paralleling process supervision~\cite{lightman2023letsverify} where step-level verification enables error localization. Supplementary equivalence analysis appears in Appendix~\ref{app:tost_sensitivity}.

Oracle analysis on SWE-bench Verified (500 instances) shows that per-test advantage concentrates at $T \geq 2$ fail-to-pass tests (31.3\% of instances, contributing 55.3\% of the total oracle advantage; Appendix~\ref{app:oracle}).

