\begin{abstract}
Execution-free verification for LLM-based code repair can be formulated as per-test binary classification (\cls{}), execution output generation (\cwm{}), or trajectory-level patch scoring, yet no controlled comparison exists across formulations. We evaluate all three on the same source data with formulation-appropriate label granularity at controlled model sizes (4--8B LoRA fine-tuning).

At function level, formulations achieve near-equivalent accuracy (\cls{} 88.4\% vs.\ \cwm{} 87.2\% at 4B, 5 seeds; Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) confirm variant-level equivalence between per-test and trajectory formulations at $p{=}0.007$). At repository level, divergence amplifies to 19--26\pp{}, with \cwm{} falling below majority baseline due to format non-compliance and output space complexity. Cross-model validation (Qwen3, DeepSeek-Coder) confirms this pattern is consistent across both families but model-contingent in magnitude.

Two findings generalize. First, per-test classification provides diagnostic granularity (which tests fail) at comparable accuracy at function level, with a configuration-dependent aggregation cost at repository level and the advantage concentrating in multi-failure instances. Second, formulations exhibit complementary errors (oracle ensemble: 90.63\%). \cls{} achieves the highest or equivalent accuracy across conditions, but aggregation penalty and error complementarity motivate complexity-aware routing.
\end{abstract}

